{
  "id": "grass-tuft",
  "name": "Grass clump",
  "notes": "One clump of pasture grass \u2014 **an assembly of 32 grass-blade ASSEMBLIES** (docs/71), which is what Robin said it should be: *\"a clump of grass should be an assembly of multiple 'grass blade' assemblies.\"* It was 32 slabs it described itself, and before that a single hexagonal column.\n\nNOTHING CHANGED BUT THE STRUCTURE. Every number is the one the flat version derived and the blade now carries: leaf area LAI 3.0 x pi R^2 = 0.033929 m^2 over 32 blades of length 0.35 m fixes the width at 3.03 mm (measured 2-3 mm for Lolium perenne); the root ring is leaf area / (2 pi H) = 15.43 mm because the blades must FIT around it.\n\n\u2605 THE SPLAY WAS RE-SOLVED for the composed geometry: k = 0.123680. The flat version's k = 0.123475 was solved against its own rounded part offsets, and composing a contained blade's placement with the blade's own offset lands 0.07 mm short of R \u2014 nothing physically, but it thinned the pasture by 0.24% and cost two plants out of a 1,200 budget, which is how it was found. The crown an assembly REPORTS must be the crown its leaf area was derived from.\n\nWHAT THE STRUCTURE BUYS: a blade is one thing the engine knows, and a meadow, a clump and a haystack are all piles of it \u2014 the same member in two conditions (`grass-blade`, `grass-blade-dry`). Damage indexes the same 32 entries it did as parts, so nothing downstream had to learn a new shape.",
